<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abbey of Saint-Victor, Marseille</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 415–</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> · Also known as Saint-Victor Abbey</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Abbey of Saint-Victor in Marseille, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, was founded around 415 by John Cassian near the tomb of the martyr Victor. Rebuilt after Saracen raids, it became one of the most powerful monasteries of southern France in the 11th century, with dependent priories across Provence, Catalonia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Its fortified church and crypts survive.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Great monasteries included Saint-Maurice d’Agaune, <b>Saint-Victor of Marseille</b>, Lérins, Montmajour near Arles and the Cluniac priory of Payerne, associated with Queen Bertha.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its judges ruled the largest of the four Sardinian realms, granting land and churches to Italian and French monasteries such as Montecassino and <b>Saint-Victor of Marseille</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/judicate-of-cagliari">Judicate of Cagliari</a></li></ul></section>
